BT
/F2 20 Tf
72 720 Td
(The outer layer: sclera and cornea) Tj
/F1 12 Tf
16 TL
0 -34 Td
(The sclera is the tough, white outer coat that covers about five sixths of the) Tj
T*
(eyeball. It is made of densely packed collagen fibres arranged irregularly,) Tj
T*
(which makes it strong but opaque. The six extraocular muscles that move the) Tj
T*
(eye attach to the sclera, and at the back it is pierced by the optic nerve.) Tj
T*
T*
(At the front, the sclera continues into the cornea, the transparent dome that) Tj
T*
(covers the iris and pupil. The cornea has no blood vessels; it takes oxygen) Tj
T*
(from the air and nutrients from tears and aqueous humor. Because of its curved) Tj
T*
(shape it provides about two thirds of the eye's total focusing power.) Tj
T*
T*
(The cornea has five classic layers: the epithelium, Bowman's layer, the) Tj
T*
(stroma, Descemet's membrane and the endothelium. The stroma makes up about 90) Tj
T*
(percent of its thickness. The endothelium pumps water out of the stroma to) Tj
T*
(keep it clear, and in humans these cells do not regenerate.) Tj
T*
T*
(The ring where cornea meets sclera is the limbus. It holds the stem cells that) Tj
T*
(renew the corneal surface and lies close to the drainage channels for aqueous) Tj
T*
(humor.) Tj
T*
T*
ET
endstream
endobj
