BT
/F2 20 Tf
72 720 Td
(The retina: where vision begins) Tj
/F1 12 Tf
16 TL
0 -34 Td
(The retina lines the back of the eye and has about ten microscopic layers. Its) Tj
T*
(outermost layer, the retinal pigment epithelium, supports the photoreceptors) Tj
T*
(and recycles their visual pigments. Oddly, the photoreceptors face away from) Tj
T*
(the incoming light, which must pass through the other retinal layers first.) Tj
T*
T*
(There are two kinds of photoreceptor. About 120 million rods are very) Tj
T*
(sensitive to light and handle night and peripheral vision, but do not detect) Tj
T*
(colour. About 6 million cones work in bright light and provide sharp, colour) Tj
T*
(vision through three types tuned to short, medium and long wavelengths.) Tj
T*
T*
(Signals pass from photoreceptors to bipolar cells and then to ganglion cells,) Tj
T*
(while horizontal and amacrine cells connect neighbouring pathways sideways.) Tj
T*
(The axons of roughly 1.2 million ganglion cells gather at the optic disc and) Tj
T*
(leave the eye as the optic nerve.) Tj
T*
T*
(The macula is a small yellowish area near the centre of the retina. At its) Tj
T*
(centre, the fovea contains densely packed cones and gives the sharpest vision.) Tj
T*
(The optic disc has no photoreceptors at all, which creates a blind spot in) Tj
T*
(each eye that the brain fills in.) Tj
T*
T*
ET
endstream
endobj
